#h2{Text}

Paragraphs are separated by blank lines. Inside them you can write #b{bold}, #i{italic}, #em{emphasis},
#code{inline code} or `inline code`, #mark{highlights}, #s{struck text}, H#sub{2}O, E = mc#sup{2}, keys
like #kbd{Ctrl} + #kbd{C}, and abbreviations such as #abbr{HTML HyperText Markup Language}. A line
break#br{}goes like this. The characters `#`, `{`, `}` and backticks go between backticks.

Links take the URL first: #link{https://github.com/PratikDeoghare/brashtag the brashtag repository}.
Links to other posts use their slug, like #ref{welcome}, and are checked when the site is built. A URL with
a fragment goes between backticks: #link{`https://go.dev/doc/#learning` learning Go}.

#quote{
  Let us change our traditional attitude to the construction of programs: Instead of imagining that our main
  task is to instruct a computer what to do, let us concentrate rather on explaining to human beings what we
  want a computer to do.
  #by{Donald E. Knuth, #cite{Literate Programming}}
}

#h2{Lists}

#ul{
  - Each line is an item.
  - A line may start with a dash, as in Markdown.
  #ul{
    - A nested list sits on a line of its own.
  }
  - Items can hold #b{markup} and `code, with commas`.
}

#ol{
  First
  Second
  Third
}

The comma separated list of the original barkdown still works, and like every list it is a block of its own:

#list{one, two, three}

